iew": "Governor Wes Moore seeks a second term in a state that leans Democratic by 15 points, and he faces Dan Cox again after defeating him by roughly 32 points in 2022. Moore has raised about $15.5 million and holds $6.5 million on hand, while Cox has raised roughly $242,000. The only recent poll, from early August, showed Moore ahead 58-31 among likely voters. Every major race rater labels the contest Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets put Moore's chances near 97 percent.\n\nThe campaign has centered on fall debates and disputes over data centers, where Moore issued an executive order that drew attention in late September. Cox would need a dramatic shift in fundamentals to close a gap this wide, and little in the money, ratings, or polling suggests one is forming.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9942, "margin": 20.0}]}